<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    ★ THE TIER 3 RUNTIME SUITE ★

    Eleven assemblies declare net472/net48 support (ADR-002), and until this
    project existed not one line of them had ever EXECUTED there. Every test
    project targeted net10.0 only, so Tier 3 was compile-verified and nothing
    more.

    That matters because ADR-002's cost has been paid seven times — portable
    equivalents for IsExternalInit, DateOnly, Index/Range, static HashData,
    [GeneratedRegex], ReadOnlySpan in a zero-dependency assembly, and RSASSA-PSS
    — and Phase 24f spent the benefit, on the claim that laboratory device hosts
    still run .NET Framework. A claim nobody had run.

    "It compiles" and "it works" differ most on exactly the constructs a
    portable equivalent replaces: culture-sensitive formatting, Substring vs
    Range, instance vs static hash APIs.

    Deliberately NARROW. It references only assemblies that reach Tier 3, and
    runs on net48 AND net10.0 so a divergence is visible as a difference
    between two runs of the same assertions rather than as an absence.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net48;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Edpf.Abstractions\Edpf.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Edpf.Core\Edpf.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Edpf.Compatibility\Edpf.Compatibility.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Edpf.Devices\Edpf.Devices.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
